### 🔴 SECURITY — found while checking gitignore, needs YOUR decision

**`github.com/sriharshaguthikonda/Tampermonkey` is PUBLIC and has raw ChatGPT conversation captures committed and pushed.**

Tracked and live on `origin/master`, `origin/enhance-tts-functionality`, `origin/nav-trail-redo` — 31 files, including:

| file | size |
|---|---|
| `Plab 2 Feedback v3 - Think deeper.html` | 772K |
| `v2 Lighthouse keeper's promise story.html` | 684K |
| `chatgpt with extenstion.html` | 680K |
| `chatgpt without extensions.html` | 636K |
| `chatgpt Lighthouse Keeper's Promise.html` | 148K |
| + 23 asset files (PNGs, a DALL·E logo image, CSS) | |

I scanned for credential patterns (counts only — I did not print or store any values). Each of the three I checked contains:
- `accessToken` × 1
- an email address × 1
- `user_id` × 1–2

ℹ️ Your own `.gitignore:45` already says *"raw DOM captures are secrets (session tokens, user identity) - never commit"*. These predate that rule, so the rule never applied to them — `.gitignore` does not affect already-tracked files. That is exactly why they slipped through.

ℹ️ Note the PLAB file is medical-exam material and likely personal to you.

👉 **I have NOT touched this.** Removing it means rewriting published history and force-pushing a public repo — destructive, irreversible, and it breaks anyone's existing clones. Your call. Options:

1. **Rotate first, clean second (what I'd do).** Log out of all ChatGPT sessions to invalidate that `accessToken` immediately — that is the only part an attacker can *use*, and it costs you nothing. Then decide about history at your leisure.
2. **Purge history** with `git filter-repo` (or BFG) across all three branches, then force-push. Removes them properly, but rewrites published commits. GitHub may still serve old blobs via cached views until you ask support to GC them.
3. **`git rm --cached` only.** Removes them going forward, leaves every past commit intact and public. Half a fix — the data stays reachable.
4. **Make the repo private.** Fastest containment, but changes how you use it, and anyone who already cloned still has the data.

Tell me which and I'll do it. Until you say so I'm leaving it completely alone and staying on the driftwatch/completion-wait work.

